===Etymology===
First attested in 1541 as {{m|en|labdomen}}. Borrowed from {{bor|en|frm|abdomen}} as well as from its etymon {{bor|en|la|abdōmen}}, possibly from {{m|la|abdō||conceal}}, from {{m|la|ab||away}} + {{m|la|-dō|t=put, place}}.<ref name=OED>{{R:OED Online|entry=abdomen|pos=n|code=abdomen_n}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈæb.də.mən/|/æbˈdəʊ.mən/|a=RP}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈæb.də.mən/|/æbˈdoʊ.mən/|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-abdomen.ogg|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|en-ca-abdomen.ogg|a=CA}}
* {{rhymes|en|əʊmən|s=3}}
* {{q|accentuation}} The variation in stress reflects two distinct historical pathways: the penultimate stress preserves the accentuation of the classical Latin etymon {{m|la|abdōmen}}, whereas the modern dominant antepenultimate stress represents a naturalized rhythmic shift originating from the Middle French borrowing with ultimate stress, reinforced by the standard accentuation of English three-syllable nouns.

===Noun===
{{en-noun|s|abdomina}}

# {{lb|en|obsolete}} The fat surrounding the belly. {{defdate|mid 16th c. – late 17th c.}}<ref name=SOED>{{R:SOED5|page=3}}</ref>
# {{senseid|en|Q9597}}{{lb|en|anatomy}} The [[belly]], or that part of the body between the [[thorax]] and the [[pelvis]], not including the back; or in some lower vertebrates, the portion between the [[cardiac]] and [[caudal]] regions. {{defdate|from early 17th c.}}<ref name=SOED/>
#: {{syn|en|belly|tummy|stomach|q3=informal|Thesaurus:belly}}
# {{lb|en|anatomy}} The cavity of the belly, which is lined by the [[peritoneum]], and contains the [[viscera]]; often restricted in humans to the part between the [[diaphragm]] and the [[commencement]] of the pelvis, the [[remainder]] being called the [[pelvic cavity]]. {{defdate|from early 17th c.}}<ref name=SOED/>
#: {{ux|en|He was all bent over complaining of pains in the '''abdomen'''.}}
# {{lb|en|zoology|entomology}} The [[posterior]] section of the body, behind the thorax, in insects, [[crustacean]]s, and other {{taxfmt|Arthropoda|phylum}}. {{defdate|from late 18th c.}}<ref name=SOED/>

